RESULT_LINE_BYTES = 512

USAGE = """tests — the test sprint's mechanical steps: the window the last test
sprint's stamp opens, the scenario list, the runs, the plan, the tasks
a run files, the stop check, the completion report, and the stamp.

  tests window [--json]
      What changed since the stamp: the files outside .ok-planner/
      save the design corpus, the sprints closed since, the stories
      changed, the scenarios whose story is gone, the test files no
      scenario claims, the claimed tests a person edited, and the test
      files an earlier plan kept outside its scope, which the window
      lists apart and counts as no reason to plan.
  tests scenarios [--json]
      The scenario list as the last stamp recorded it.
  tests declare [--run <command>] [--silence <seconds>] [--named yes|no]
                [--leftovers <command>] [--slowest <n>]
                [--runners [<command> ...]] [--setup <command> | none]
                [--setup-silence <seconds>]
      Write the keys given into testing in .ok-planner/config.json, keep
      the others, and drop the earlier testing.timeout. run is the suite
      command, holding {tests} and {results}; silence, the whole seconds
      with no result after which a run stops; named, whether the suite
      command runs a test by its name inside its file; leftovers, a
      command that prints one JSON object counting each kind of resource
      a test may leave behind, {} where it counts none; slowest, how
      many of the slowest tests each run lists; runners, the project's
      own test-running commands, which review checks refuses as a check,
      none where it names no runner of its own; setup, a command each
      run runs before the suite command to get the test environment
      ready, safe to run again and fast where the environment is ready,
      or none, written null, where the project needs no setup; and
      setup_silence, the whole seconds with no output after which setup
      stops, which a declared setup needs. --timeout is refused: a run
      stops after testing.silence seconds with no result or output
      instead.
  tests run [--sprint <path> [--failing]] [--only <scenario> ...] [--from <file>] [--json]
      Run the tests in one run of testing.run, the project's suite
      command. {tests} becomes the path of a JSON file, {"workers": <n>,
      "tests": [{"path", "name", "resources": [...]}]}, and {results} the
      path of a stream the command writes one line to per test as each
      finishes: {"path", "name", "passed": true or false, "seconds":
      <number>, "output": "<text>"}. The command writes its lines to that
      path and never replaces the file. Several workers may write it at
      once, so each line, newline included, stays within %d bytes, the
      least PIPE_BUF POSIX allows: cap output to fit. A test's first
      line stands; a later line, or one that is not JSON or lacks path,
      passed, or seconds, is unmatched; a test with no line fails.
      Where testing.setup is a command, the run runs it first, with no
      clock but testing.setup_silence seconds with no output, and starts
      the suite command only after setup exits 0; a failed setup fails
      every test with a tail that opens setup failed. The run stops
      after testing.silence seconds with no result line and no output,
      or, while a test that records a silence of its own has no result,
      after the longest such silence. Without
      --sprint, the tests the stamp recorded; with --sprint, the tests
      the test sprint plans, each result appended to the round beside it
      as it lands, with the plan's budget workers, one where the plan has
      none, each test with the shared resources it declares. Around a
      round, testing.leftovers runs before and after, and the round
      records both counts and each kind whose count grew, which fails
      the round. A run that records a round refuses while another run
      records a round of the same test sprint. --failing reruns only the tests the latest round lists
      as failing with no held promise or owner issue, as a round of kind
      rerun; it refuses where no round is recorded or that round lists
      no failing test, which sends you to the whole suite as the gate,
      and takes no --only or --from. --only and --from run with one
      worker, and record no round; --only prints the testing.leftovers
      counts around its run; --from runs the tests a JSON list of {path,
      name, scenario} names. It prints each result, then the
      testing.slowest slowest tests by what each adds to the run's wall
      time: a test that declares shared resources runs holding them
      alone and adds its whole seconds, and an isolated test adds its
      seconds divided by the run's workers. Every slowest list this
      tool prints or reads is ranked this way, a round's from the
      workers and resources its header records, or, for a round an
      earlier release recorded without them, the plan's budget workers
      and the resources each live test declares.
  tests groups --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run> [--size <n>]
               [--widen [<story slug> ...]] [--json]
      Write the planning groups beside the test sprint, -groups.json in
      place of .md, and print each group's key, its stories with the
      reasons each is touched, and its brief, and the inputs every
      planner's brief carries. It also lists the test files to sort:
      each test file no scenario claims, each claimed test a person
      edited, marked claimed, and each test file an earlier plan kept,
      that lies inside the run's scope; and it lists as kept, left as
      they stand, the ones outside the scope. Where the window's status is new, every
      story is touched. Otherwise a story is touched where its file
      changed since the stamp, where a file changed since the stamp
      cites it with @story:, where its recorded test failed in the
      planning run's results, or, with the reason slow <scenario>
      (<seconds>s), where its recorded test is among the
      testing.slowest slowest there, ranked with the stamp's budget
      workers. --widen touches each story it
      names, with the reason widen, on any window; with no slug, each
      story whose earlier calls, as `tests calls` lists them, left a
      path out. Stories one file cites join one group of up to --size
      stories (8 where absent), split in catalog order; the
      other touched stories are batched by that size in catalog order.
      It refuses a run with no scope item, a run with a program
      question the owner has not answered, and a --widen slug with no
      story file.
  tests calls <story slug> ... [--json]
      For each story, the archived test sprint whose plan planned it
      last, newest first by the closed: stamps, and that plan's calls
      for the story that left a path out: each call of kind untested,
      or every call of the story in a plan whose calls carry no kind.
      A plan that records no planned stories planned every story. A
      call belongs to a story by its story field, else by a site or
      key that is the story's slug or starts with <slug>--.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run>
      Assemble the test sprint's plan from the planners' items, the
      run's one scope item, and the groups `tests groups` wrote, check
      it, and write it beside the test sprint. It takes each story's
      items from the latest done story task that records a stories item
      for it, keeps each recorded scenario of a story no group lists,
      and of a story whose every reason is widen or slow each recorded
      scenario its planner names nowhere, and refuses a touched story
      no done story task records, and a run with no groups. It refuses a run with no scope item or more than one, and
      each new or rewritten scenario with no entry, kind, or setup,
      with a test path, support file, or entry outside the scope, or
      with an entry the owner left unguarded. It refuses a legacy entry
      whose path lies outside the scope, a claimed test file to sort that
      no sort agent sorted, and a test file to sort that no scenario
      claims and the sort names nowhere, and records the test files
      outside the scope as kept. It takes the budget and its
      limits from the latest done budget task, and refuses one that
      records no budget item or more than one, workers that is not a
      whole number of 1 or more, a budget that names no stack or no
      sizing, a limit handled other than by raise, split, or lease, and
      a scenario's resources that is not a list of slugs. It takes the
      time controls from the controls items, one per id, and refuses
      two that record one id with different fields, a new or rewritten
      scenario with no waits or one that is none of event: <what the
      product emits>, control: <a time control>, and clock: <the clock
      the test moves>, a new or rewritten scenario's waits that names a
      control--<slug> no item records, and a time control whose form is not trigger, clock, or
      setting, that names no site or no files, that names no gate where
      it is a trigger or a clock or one where it is a setting, or that
      no scenario waits on. It keeps each fixture a kept test uses as
      that test's support and deletes each one no kept test uses, and
      refuses a deletion or port of a file a kept scenario names as
      support, a support entry that names no test that reads it, a
      port of rule inferred that states no inferred rule, inferred text
      beside any other sort or rule, and a claimed test file a person
      edited that no sort agent sorted.
      Where testing.named is false, an adopted scenario's test is its
      whole file.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run> --questions
      The questions that no answer settles yet, from the planner tasks
      whose items the assembly takes, as JSON.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --show [--json]
      Print the plan: a summary first (scenarios per story by action,
      the untested and departure calls whole, the other calls by kind,
      each inferred rule a port enforces with its test and check, and
      the planned tests by kind and setup), then the counts and
      the listing, each shared resource with its tests, the budget, and
      each time control the plan builds. --json prints the plan file as
      it stands.
  tests tasks --sprint <path> --stage <write | sweep | fix | verify | speed>
      File the run's tasks for one stage into its task run. Where the
      plan lists time controls, the write stage first files one control
      task per time control alone, refiles one that closed short of
      done, refuses where one closed blocked twice, and files the rest
      only once each closed done, each after every control task. Where
      the plan's budget names a test stack, the write stage then files the
      preflight task alone, refiles it where it closed short of done,
      refuses where it closed blocked twice, and files the rest only
      once it closed done. The write stage files a support task per
      support file the written scenarios name, then the test tasks,
      which wait on them, then the legacy tasks, and refiles each test
      task that closed blocked on a helpers item after the support task
      that adds the helper, and refiles the test task of each scenario
      whose latest tests item a verifier or a speed reviewer sent back,
      with the reason, after any task that holds its files. The fix stage files one fix
      task per failure tests failures lists, a grown leftover count
      included. The verify stage files one verify task per scenario
      whose latest tests item records a test and stands open, with no
      task before it, and one per fixes or promises item that stands
      open, each after the last. The speed stage walks the latest
      round's tests that this sprint's test tasks wrote or modified,
      ranked by what each adds to the run's wall time, testing.slowest
      at a time. It files one speed task per test in the first batch
      that holds a test no speed item accepts at its digest, a hash of
      its test file and the support files the plan records for it, so a
      rewrite clears an acceptance. It walks past a batch whose tests
      are all accepted where a speed review sent any of them back, and
      stops at a batch whose tests were all accepted with no send-back,
      or at the end of the list. It refuses where the latest round is a
      rerun round, lists a failure, or ran before a changing task
      closed, and where a tests item stands sent back or awaits its
      verifier. It refiles a speed task that closed short of done or
      with no verdict, and refuses where two did.
  tests ports --sprint <path>
      Add each project check the latest legacy task of each file
      recorded to the review loop's checks. It writes nothing, and
      refuses, where checks is not a list of commands or holds one that
      starts a test runner, and where a recorded command starts a test
      runner, as review checks finds one, naming each such ports item,
      the legacy task that recorded it, and the runner, and saying to
      refile that task with the refusal.
  tests snapshot --sprint <path> --key <fix key> [--after] <file> ...
      Keep each file as it stands before a fixer first edits it, or,
      with --after, as the fixer leaves it when it closes.
  tests diff --sprint <path> --key <fix key>
      The change one fixer made, from its two snapshots. Filing the
      next attempt for a sent-back fix backs that fix's change out.
  tests stray --sprint <path> [--json]
      The test files, and the support files the stamp or the plan
      records, that no planned test claims as its own or as support.
  tests failures --sprint <path> [--json]
      The latest round's failing tests that no held promise and no
      owner issue settles, and leftovers--<kind> for each kind of
      leftover whose count grew in it.
  tests report --sprint <path>
      Print the test sprint's completion report, with each round's
      slowest tests and each speed review's verdict.
  tests complete --sprint <path>
      Check the completion contract and print TESTS COMPLETE or TESTS
      NOT COMPLETE. The latest round must be a whole-suite round, closed,
      run after every changing task closed, with no grown leftover
      count; a rerun round as the latest fails it. Each time control
      the plan lists needs its control task closed done and its
      controls item. No scenario's latest tests item may await its
      verifier or stand sent back. The speed stage's walk over the
      latest round must reach its stop with every test it walked
      accepted by a speed item at its current digest. TESTS COMPLETE writes the mark beside the test
      sprint, -complete.txt in place of .md, that turns the lint's test
      check back on; TESTS NOT COMPLETE removes it.
  tests stamp --sprint <archived path> --commit <sha>
      After the owner commits the run's work: write the scenario list
      the run leaves, from the latest whole-suite round, and each
      inferred rule the run ported, {rule, check, command, test,
      sprint}, beside the ones earlier stamps recorded, and the test
      files the plan kept outside its scope, stamp it with <sha>, and
      add closed: <sha> to the archived test sprint.
  tests rules [--json]
      Each inferred rule a stamp recorded that no take settled and whose
      command still stands in the review loop's checks. It needs no
      testing config, and prints none where no stamp recorded one.
  tests rules take <check> --as practice:<slug> | retired
      Settle the inferred rule of one check: as the practice
      .ok-planner/practices/<slug>.md, which must exist, or retired. It
      refuses a check no stamp recorded, and one already settled.

The scenario list lives in .ok-planner/test-state.json, which only
this tool writes: the stamp, each scenario with its test, its support
files, what its test waits on, and the shared resources its test
declares it changes, the plan's budget, each program the owner chose to leave unguarded,
and each inferred rule a port carries, with how it was settled.
A scenario that records no resources declares none, and a plan or a
stamp with no budget runs one worker. A tests item that lists
resources keeps only the planned ones it names, so a rewrite may drop
a declaration and never adds one.

Exit 0: done (TESTS COMPLETE under complete). Exit 2: TESTS NOT
COMPLETE. Exit 1: usage, input, or read error.""" % RESULT_LINE_BYTES

